  "companions": {
    "note_beginner": "Good companions for squash do one of three things: draw pests away, bring in bees, or feed the soil. Plant a few nasturtiums or marigolds nearby to lure squash bugs off your plants, add flowers like borage to attract the bees that pollinate your squash, and let beans share the bed to enrich the soil. Just give your squash plenty of room, since the plants get big.",
    "sources": [
      "umn_ext"
    ],
    "anchoring_urls": {
      "umn_ext": {
        "url": "https://extension.umn.edu/planting-and-growing-guides/companion-planting-home-gardens",
        "verified": "2026-06-23"
      }
    },
    "note_seasoned": "Squash benefits most from companions that pull pests away, feed beneficial insects, or fix nitrogen, rather than from plants packed in tight. Give sprawling vines their room and use companions as a border or light interplanting. The strongest evidence is for nasturtium, with marigold close behind, reducing squash bug and cucumber beetle pressure, while the Three Sisters trio (corn, beans, squash) earns its place through nitrogen, support, and pest confusion. Pollinator flowers like borage and sweet alyssum matter because squash is insect-pollinated, so anything that keeps bees working improves fruit set. Several herb and flower pairings here are traditional rather than trial-proven, and are labeled accordingly.",
    "good_beginner": [],
    "good_beginner_seasoned": [
      {
        "name": "Corn",
        "why_beginner": "Part of the classic Three Sisters planting. The tall stalks support climbing beans, and growing corn, beans, and squash together can confuse squash pests."
      },
      {
        "name": "Pole beans",
        "why_beginner": "Beans pull nitrogen from the air and feed the soil, which hungry squash appreciate. Let them climb the corn in a Three Sisters bed."
      },
      {
        "name": "Nasturtium",
        "why_beginner": "These easy flowers lure squash bugs and beetles away from your squash, and research backs it up. Bonus: the flowers are edible."
      }
    ],
