  "start_method": {
    "start": "both",
    "weeks_before": 3,
    "hardening_off_seasoned": "Harden off indoor-started squash over 7 to 10 days, easing them into direct sun and wind before transplanting. Handle the rootball gently at planting, because squash resents root disturbance and a checked seedling can sulk for weeks.",
    "hardening_off_beginner": "If you started seedlings indoors, set them outside for a little longer each day over about a week so they adjust to sun and wind. Be gentle with the roots when you plant, since squash does not like having its roots disturbed.",
    "notes_seasoned": "Because squash resents root disturbance, direct sowing once the soil warms past 60°F, ideally 70°F, is the simplest and most reliable method, and direct-sown plants quickly catch up to transplants. Start seeds indoors only to gain two to three weeks in a short season, using larger cells (3 inch or bigger) and setting plants out before the roots circle the pot.",
    "notes_beginner": "The easiest way to grow zucchini is to sow seeds right where they will grow, once the soil is warm (about 70°F). Squash grows fast and does not transplant happily, so direct sowing usually works best. Start seeds indoors only if your season is short, and move them outside while they are still small."
  },
