  "varieties": {
    "recommended": [
      {
        "name": "Black Beauty",
        "recommended_note": "Classic dark-green heirloom; compact, open-pollinated, and dependably productive."
      },
      {
        "name": "Costata Romanesco",
        "recommended_note": "Ribbed Italian heirloom prized for nutty flavor; large, sprawling plants that reward room."
      },
      {
        "name": "Raven",
        "recommended_note": "Compact dark-green hybrid that suits containers and small beds."
      },
      {
        "name": "Goldrush",
        "recommended_note": "Bright golden-yellow hybrid with uniform, easy-to-spot fruit."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cocozelle",
        "recommended_note": "Striped Italian heirloom with firm, flavorful fruit."
      },
      {
        "name": "Dunja",
        "recommended_note": "Vigorous organic hybrid with an open, easy-to-pick habit."
      }
    ],
    "note_seasoned": "For a first garden almost any zucchini succeeds, so choose by space and color. Compact bush habits like Raven or Dunja fit small beds and containers and keep airflow open to slow powdery mildew, while sprawling heirlooms like Costata Romanesco and Cocozelle reward more room with exceptional flavor. Mixing a green type with a yellow one such as Goldrush makes fruit easier to spot before it oversizes. Black Beauty remains the reliable green standard.",
    "note_beginner": "Zucchini is forgiving, so pick a variety by the space you have. Bush types like Raven stay compact and do well in pots, while classics like Black Beauty are reliable in a garden bed. A yellow type such as Goldrush is easy to spot at picking size.",
    "sources": [
      "umn_ext",
      "umd_ext"
    ],
