    "trigger": "last_frost",
    "tip_seasoned": "Zucchini is famous for overwhelming a garden, so resist sowing the whole crop at once. A first sowing after your last frost, then a second about three weeks later, spreads the harvest and keeps vigorous plants coming as the originals tire. The real payoff is late season: squash vine borer and powdery mildew often take down the first planting by midsummer, so a replacement sown in early to midsummer, counting back from your first fall frost so it has time to mature, frequently outproduces the spring crop on cleaner, less stressed plants.",
    "tip_beginner": "You rarely need more than one or two zucchini plants at a time, and a single planting can feed you for weeks. For a steadier supply, sow a few seeds now and a few more about three weeks later. A second planting in summer is a smart backup, since pests and disease often wear out the first plants by late summer."
